    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4350A\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act relating to establishing Angelina Liu as the genetic child and\ndistributee of Wenjian Liu for purposes of the estates, powers and\ntrusts law\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo amend the Estates, Powers and Trusts Law (EPTL) to establish that\nAngelina Liu shall be considered the genetic child of Wenjian Liu for\npurposes of intestate succession\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill provides that Angelina Liu shall be considered the\ngenetic child and distributee of Wenjian Liu despite any provisions of\nsection 4-1.3 of EPTL to the contrary.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nInterested parties who want to determine if the child of a decedent\nshould inherit an estate when the child is posthumously conceived using\nthe genetic material of the decedent can normally turn to Section 4-1.3\nof the Estates, Powers and Trusts Law (EPTL) for guidance. This section\noutlines several requirements that must be met before the child can be\nconsidered \"biological\": 1) the child must be born within 33 months of\nthe decedent's death, 2) the decedent must have provided a written\ninstrument consenting to the use of his/her genetic material and author-\nizing a person to make decisions about that material, 3) the person\nauthorized must then record this written instrument with the Surrogate\nCourt within seven months of the death, and 4) the written instrument\nmust have been signed by the decedent in the presence of two witnesses.\nWhile these requirements may be entirely reasonable for instances in\nwhich a death is anticipated, they fail to account for instances in\nwhich it is not.\n\nTo demonstrate this point, let us look no further than the case of NYPD\nDetective Wenjian Liu, who was tragically murdered in his vehicle on\nDecember 20th, 2014 while on duty with his partner Detective Rafael\nRamos. Det. Liu and his wife Pei Xia Chen had discussed having children\nprior to his death, and so the hospital set aside Det. Liu's genetic\nmaterial so that Ms. Chen could pursue in-vitro fertilization at a later\ndate if she so chose. During the month of July 2017, more than 2 1/2\nyears after Det. Liu's death, Ms.  Chen gave birth to her daughter Ange-\nlina. When Ms. Chen went to apply for federal Social Security benefits,\nhowever, she was denied under a federal law which dictates that the\nchild must be born or conceived prior to the genetic parent's death in\norder to receive benefits. If this is not the case, the child is then\nconsidered non-marital and the Social Security Administration (SSA)\nturns to applicable state law to determine the biological status of the\nchild - and this is where EPTL 4-1.3 comes into play. In the case at\nhand, Det. Liu and his wife meet only one of the four requirements\noutlined above as the child was born within 33 months of Det. Liu's\ndeath. With a death so sudden and unexpected, however, there was no\nopportunity for the Liu family to meet any of the other requirements in\nthe statute.\n\nThis legislation provides a pathway for the Liu family to establish\nAngelina as the biological heir of Wenjian Liu so that she may receive\nSocial Security benefits and any other rights and privileges duly\naccorded under Article 3 of the Estates, Powers and Trusts Law.  The\nsponsor believes the extraordinary circumstances of this case merit an\nexception to the stringent requirements of state law.  Providing Det.\nLiu's widow and child the ability to receive federal survivor's benefits\nto replace his lost wage is the very least we can do to honor the\ntremendous sacrifice he made on the night of December 20th, 2014.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall be deemed\n",
